const faqData = [
  {
    question: 'What constellations make up the Perseus family?',
    answer: 'The Perseus family contains nine constellations, most of them characters from a single Greek myth: Perseus (the hero), Andromeda (the princess he rescues), Cassiopeia (her mother), Cepheus (her father), Pegasus (the winged horse), and Cetus (the sea monster). Added to these are Auriga (the charioteer), Lacerta (the lizard), and Triangulum (the triangle). They dominate the autumn evening sky in the northern hemisphere.'
  },
  {
    question: 'What is the story behind the Perseus family?',
    answer: 'The constellations tell one connected legend. Queen Cassiopeia boasted that she — or her daughter Andromeda — was more beautiful than the sea nymphs. As punishment, the sea monster Cetus was sent to ravage the coast, and Andromeda was chained to a rock as a sacrifice. The hero Perseus, returning on the winged horse Pegasus after slaying the Gorgon Medusa, saw Andromeda, defeated Cetus, and rescued her. All the main characters were placed together in the sky.'
  },
  {
    question: 'What is the most famous object in the Perseus family?',
    answer: 'The Andromeda Galaxy (Messier 31) is the standout. At about 2.5 million light-years away, it is the most distant object visible to the unaided eye and the nearest large spiral galaxy to our own Milky Way. Under a dark sky it appears as a faint elongated smudge in the constellation Andromeda. It is on a collision course with the Milky Way and will merge with it in roughly 4.5 billion years.'
  },
  {
