    answer: 'Algol (Beta Persei) is a famous eclipsing binary star in Perseus, marking the head of Medusa. Every 2.87 days a dimmer companion passes in front of the brighter star, causing Algol to fade noticeably for several hours before recovering. Ancient observers who noticed this "winking" gave it an ominous reputation — the name comes from the Arabic for "the ghoul," and it represents the severed head of the Gorgon.'
  },
  {
    question: 'Are the Perseids meteors related to the constellation Perseus?',
    answer: 'The Perseid meteor shower, one of the best of the year, appears to radiate from the constellation Perseus, which is why it bears that name. However, the meteors have nothing physically to do with the stars of Perseus — they are tiny debris particles shed by Comet Swift–Tuttle, burning up in Earth’s atmosphere. The shower peaks around August 12–13 each year, and Perseus simply marks the direction from which the meteors seem to stream.'
  },
  {
    question: 'When is the best time to see the Perseus family?',
    answer: 'The Perseus family rules the autumn evening sky in the northern hemisphere, best placed from September through December. The Great Square of Pegasus is high overhead in October evenings, with Andromeda and Perseus trailing off to one side and Cassiopeia’s W nearly overhead. Cassiopeia and Cepheus are circumpolar from northern latitudes, so they can be seen year-round, but the full family is best in autumn.'
  }
